---
// Help for the notebook preview (STUDY-DESK.md M12, D6). A static page that
// reads without the desk: it is noindex, out of the sitemap, and (as every
// generic page) out of Pagefind. The panel's foot, the first-time choice and
// the tour link here.
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../../styles/pages/notebook-help.css";
---

<Layout
  title="Your notebook | LIT Bible"
  description="Help for the notebook preview: where it is kept, saving and opening a copy, and what each part does."
  canonical="https://litbible.net/notebook/help/"
  robotsNoindex={true}
>
  <article class="nbhelp" aria-labelledby="nbhelp-title">
    <header class="nbhelp__header">
      <div class="nbhelp__inner">
        <h1 id="nbhelp-title" class="nbhelp__title">Your notebook</h1>
        <p class="nbhelp__lede">
          The notebook is a preview of study tools for reading on a computer: notes,
          bookmarks and highlights that stay with you as you read. It is switched on
          for a few readers while it is tried out. This page explains it, and you
          can read it whether or not it is on for you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="nbhelp__body">

      <section aria-labelledby="ways-to-keep">
        <h2 id="ways-to-keep">Ways to keep your notebook</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>In this browser.</strong> Your notebook is kept in this browser, on
          this computer. Nothing is sent to the LIT Bible team or anywhere else.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What can clear it.</strong> Clearing your browser’s data for this
          site removes the notebook. So can a private window that is closed, a
          browser set to clear data when it quits, and a different browser or
          computer, which each start with an empty notebook. Your browser may also
          clear stored data on its own when it is short of room.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Safari.</strong> Safari can clear a site’s stored data after seven
          days of using Safari without visiting the site. If you use Safari, save a
          copy now and then.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Saving a copy.</strong> A copy is a file on your own computer, and
          it is the safe way to keep your notebook. When you first use the notebook
          you can ask to be reminded: the panel then tells you when it has been a
          week since you saved one. You can change that at the foot of the panel.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Other ways.</strong> Other ways to keep it, across your computers
          and the apps, are being planned. Nothing here depends on them.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="copies">
        <h2 id="copies">Saving and opening a copy</h2>
        <p>
          The <a href="/notebook/#backup">notebook page</a> can save a copy in three
          kinds of file:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A Markdown file</strong>, to read, print or move into other writing.</li>
          <li><strong>A Web Annotation file</strong> (JSON), a standard format other annotation tools can read.</li>
          <li><strong>A full backup</strong> (JSON), which holds everything and is the one to open again here.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          “Open a copy” on the same page puts a saved full backup back into the
          notebook.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="delete-everything">
        <h2 id="delete-everything">Deleting everything</h2>
        <p>
          Deleting a note, bookmark or highlight moves it to the trash for 30 days,
          and Undo (or Ctrl+Z, ⌘Z on a Mac) brings it back. To remove the whole
          notebook from this browser, use “Delete everything” on the
          <a href="/notebook/">notebook page</a>; “Empty the trash now” is there
          too. Clearing this site’s data in your browser also removes it. Save a
          copy first if you may want it back.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="places">
        <h2 id="places">Places and ribbons</h2>
        <p>
          A place is a spot you want to return to, and a ribbon marks it in the text, so
          you can find your way back to it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="labels">
        <h2 id="labels">Labels</h2>
        <p>
          Labels are your own words for sorting what you keep. Give a note,
          highlight or bookmark a label, and filter by it in the panel and on the
          notebook page. They belong to you; they are not the topics the site uses
          for its own chapters.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="greek">
        <h2 id="greek">The Greek tab</h2>
        <p>
          On a Study View chapter, the notebook’s Greek tab shows the chapter in the
          Greek of the SBL Greek New Testament (SBLGNT), in its own paragraphs. It
          opens at the verse you are reading, and a “Go to verse” button brings you
          back to it. Like the rest of the notebook, it is for computers.
        </p>
        <p>
          Click a word, or move through the words with the arrow keys, to see its
          details at the foot of the tab: its dictionary form, its parsing in words,
          its Strong’s number and an entry from a lexicon. You can choose between the
          UBS Dictionary of the Greek New Testament and STEPBible’s brief lexicon,
          which shows the full LSJ entry under its own. Your choice is remembered in this
          browser.
        </p>
        <p>
          The details also list the LIT’s footnote letters for that verse, which open
          “This verse,” and, for a word the glossary covers, how the LIT renders it in
          that verse. A text-critical sign in the Greek opens the apparatus at the
          end of the text, which compares Westcott and Hort, Tregelles, NA28 and
          Robinson–Pierpont. “This verse” also shows the Greek of the verse you are on.
        </p>
        <p>
          To search the Greek, use the search button at the top of the tab, or the
          site’s search box. Type a Greek word, with or without accents, to find every
          form of it, or a Strong’s number such as G4102; the site’s search then shows
          the Greek alone. To search by a transliteration such as pistis, or by an
          English word, choose “Greek” in the search’s type menu (the tab’s search is
          always the Greek). An English word finds the Greek the LIT renders that way
          for the glossary’s terms, such as justness or self-preservation, then the
          Greek words a lexicon glosses that way, such as faith. In a word’s details, its form, its dictionary form
          and its Strong’s number each start a search too. A result in another chapter
          opens that chapter with the Greek tab at the word.
        </p>
        <p>
          A lexicon may give a different sense from the LIT. That is expected. The
          LIT’s footnotes give the reasons for its own choices.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Greek data is shared under the licenses of its sources. They are listed
          under “Sources and licenses” at the foot of the tab.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="search">
        <h2 id="search">Search and commands</h2>
        <p>
          The search box also looks in your notebook and understands a few
          commands. Type “tour,” for example, to take the tour again.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="tour">
        <h2 id="tour">The tour</h2>
        <p>
          The tour walks through the notebook on a page of scripture, in three
          parts: marking the text, the Notebook, and finding and keeping. Each step
          shows the thing working, opening a verse’s menu or a tab for you, and you
          can skip a part or the whole tour. It never starts by itself, it keeps
          nothing in your notebook, and when it ends the page is as you left it.
          <a href="/john-3/?desk-tour">Take the tour</a> on John 3. It needs the
          notebook preview to be on for you.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="sharing">
        <h2 id="sharing">Sharing what you write</h2>
        <p>
          What you write is yours. If you share it with the LIT’s words quoted
          inside, such as in a study guide or a bulletin, the
          <a href="/read/#license-terms">license terms</a> say how the translation
          may be quoted.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="missing">
        <h2 id="missing">Tell us what’s missing</h2>
        <p>
          The site deliberately doesn’t measure who uses what, so we only hear what
          you tell us. If the notebook is missing something, or something is
          confusing, <a href="/contact/">write to us</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
